  model at the same time as its 200 reward  peak we can see game victories at around  70  against the hard computer bot which  isn't too bad especially considering  random was literally zero percent we  were never winning despite the amount of  effort put in so far i would still  consider this just the beginning of any  reinforcement learning with starcraft 2  and i'll have to think more on how i  might expand this to do better at both  macro tasks like we've done here as well  as some sort of micro reinforcement  learning algorithm probably in the form  of two separate algorithms running at  the same time where one algorithm is  focusing on macro like what we have here  and making large overarching decisions  like build buildings and expand or  attack and so on and then another one  that focuses solely on micro tasks for  example like what coordinates to go to  or build on and so on but overall i'd  say this is a pretty good start i did  also attempt to build some sort of  hunting capability for the void rays to  find enemies at more locations than just  their base or if they just happen to  show up since it seemed like many games  were lost because the enemy just hid  when we had the advantage but later came  with a bigger army and defeated us i  ended up allowing void rays to look at  random coordinates if no other enemies  were present but my results here were  actually worse than when we weren't  looking at all i still think there's  something here to be fixed logically  probably fairly simply but again i  wonder about maybe instead making a  reinforcement learning algorithm that  chooses what coordinates to fly to  rather than me hand coding even more  logic i think making a micro strategy  reinforcement learning algorithm to pick  coordinates for travel is very likely  the next step to try that however is a  project for another day i've hosted all  the code as well as a decent model that  you can play with if you'd like you can  find that in the description of this  video if you're interested in learning  more about neural networks and how they  work then you might want to check out  neural networks from scratch a book by  myself and daniel kukiewa inside of  which you'll learn how to code neurons  activation functions how to calculate  loss to optimization and back  propagation and of course apply  everything that you've just learned to a  problem the book itself is in full color  for graphs and code syntax highlighting  everything's on high quality pages with  full font and a comfortable line spacing  to make reading easy finally every  concept including the math and coding is  broken down into the truly atomic parts  and then put back together step by step  to give a full understanding of neural  networks and how they work including all  the math behind them by the end of the  book you will have created your own  neural network framework from scratch  this is the book that i wish existed  when i was learning deep learning and  i'm extremely happy to be able to offer  it to you if this sounds interesting to  you then you can head over to nnfs  to learn more and buy yourself a copy  you
 

